{#-
  The technical analysis: the document the tool is built from.

  Its reader is whoever builds — an AI developer under a driver's supervision — so it
  is in English, and there is no catalogue behind it: this document is never read by
  the client.

  Rendered by the analyst (`webtools_analyst/documents.py`) with Jinja2, because that
  subsystem is Python. The pre-specification's model next door is nunjucks, rendered by
  the preanalyst, which is Node. Whoever produces a document holds its template.

  What arrives here: `project_id` and `template`, which are ours, and `analysis` and
  `assumptions`, which are a model's words. The model's words go in the **body** only.
  Nothing written by a model is ever put in the front matter, where it would be YAML
  that somebody else parses.

  **There is no title above the analysis.** The policy says the model's answer is the
  document, whole, with its own headings, so a heading of ours on top would be a second
  title. What is added here is what is ours and nowhere else: the front matter, and the
  assumptions as a closing section.

  This comment's tags carry the minus sign that strips the whitespace around them, so
  the rendered file **begins** with the front matter's first dash. A document whose
  first line is anything else has no front matter as far as workspaces is concerned,
  and it would be given a fresh one with ours pushed down into the body.
-#}
